.TH CUT 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
cut \- cut out selected fields, bytes, or characters of each line of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cut
.BI \-b " list"
.RB [ \-n ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B cut
.BI \-c " list"
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B cut
.BI \-f " list"
.RB [ \-d
.IR delim ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cut
cuts out and writes to standard output the selected portion of each
line of each
.I file
\(em bytes, characters, or tab\-delimited fields, according to which
of
.BR \-b ", " \-c ", "
or
.B \-f
is given.
Exactly one of the three must be given; giving more than one, or
none, is a usage error.
.PP
In field mode
.RB ( \-f ),
a line with no delimiter character on it is passed through unmodified
unless
.B \-s
is given. In byte and character mode, the newline itself is never
selectable and is always preserved on lines that have one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-b " list"
Select the bytes named by
.I list
from each line, counted from 1.
.TP
.BI \-c " list"
Select the characters named by
.I list
from each line, counted from 1. Characters are decoded as UTF\-8; a
byte that cannot be decoded (an invalid or incomplete sequence)
falls back to a single raw byte, so a corrupt byte is never silently
dropped or allowed to desynchronize the rest of the line.
.TP
.BI \-d " delim"
Use
.I delim
instead of \fI<tab>\fR as the field delimiter. Only valid with
.BR \-f ;
combining it with
.B \-b
or
.B \-c
is a usage error.
.I delim
must be exactly one character.
.TP
.BI \-f " list"
Select the fields named by
.I list
from each line, where fields are separated by the delimiter
character (\fI<tab>\fR by default, or the argument to
.BR \-d ).
.TP
.B \-n
Accepted as a recognized option token, but always refused with a
usage error: rounding a byte selection
.RB ( \-b )
to the nearest character boundary is not implemented by this
version.
.TP
.B \-s
Suppress lines with no delimiter character on them entirely, instead
of passing them through unmodified. Only valid with
.BR \-f ;
combining it with
.B \-b
or
.B \-c
is a usage error.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I list
A comma\- or \fI<blank>\fR\-separated list of positive decimal
integers and/or ranges. An element may be:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.IR N ,
the
.IR N th
byte, character, or field, counted from 1;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR N\- ,
from the
.IR N th
to the end of the line;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR N\-M ,
from the
.IR N th
through the
.IR M th
inclusive (\fIM\fR must be at least \fIN\fR);
.IP \(bu 2
.IR \-M ,
from the first through the
.IR M th
inclusive.
.RE
.RS
The selected bytes, characters, or fields are always written in
ascending position order, regardless of the order
.I list
names them in and regardless of how many of its ranges overlap a
given position.
.RE
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is used. If no
.I file
operand is given at all, standard input is used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input files were processed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be opened or read; processing continues with the
next operand.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: no mode (or more than one) was given, an invalid
.IR list ,
.B \-n
was given,
.B \-d
or
.B \-s
was combined with
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c ,
or
.I delim
was not exactly one character.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR paste (1),
.BR tr (1),
.BR wc (1)
